    <div class="callout warning" style="margin-bottom:16px;">
      <strong>Critical Finding — Discount Effectiveness</strong>
      Average quantity per order is virtually identical across all discount tiers (2.98–3.00 items). Discounts are not driving customers to buy more units. Combined with the lower average order values at each tier, this indicates that discounts are being applied to transactions that would likely have occurred anyway — converting full-price orders into discounted ones without generating incremental volume.
    </div>

    <div class="callout-grid">
      <div class="callout info">
        <strong>Margin Stability</strong>
        Profit margins remain ~40% across all discount tiers, which suggests costs are being adjusted proportionally (or discounts are absorbed by the cost structure). This masks the true revenue cost of discounting.
      </div>
      <div class="callout warning">
        <strong>Revenue Leakage</strong>
        37.5% of orders carry discounts but generate only 33.5% of revenue. If those 18,774 discounted orders had been captured at full-price AOV ($27.15), total revenue would have been ~$509K higher — a 40% increase.
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="insight-strip">
      <strong>Strategic Recommendation:</strong> Conduct a controlled test removing or reducing the 20% discount tier for one quarter to measure true demand elasticity. Given that quantity per order is flat across all discount levels, there is strong evidence that at least part of the discounted volume represents demand that exists at full price. <strong>Reducing discount utilization by 10 percentage points could add $30K–$50K in annual revenue</strong> without volume loss.
    </div>
